# 🚀 My Prompt Engineering Journey

> Documenting my path from **complete beginner → confident prompt engineer**, one prompt at a time.
> Started: **September 2025** 📍 Chattogram, Bangladesh

---

## 🎯 Why This Repo Exists

Prompt engineering is the skill of communicating with AI models to get exactly what you want.
This repository is my **learning journal, roadmap, and playground** — every lesson, experiment,
prompt, and project lives here as I level up.

**My goals:**
- [ ] Understand how Large Language Models (LLMs) actually work
- [ ] Master core → advanced prompting techniques
- [ ] Build 3+ real projects using prompts
- [ ] Learn to evaluate and secure prompts (prompt injection defense)
- [ ] Share what I learn in public

---

## 🗺️ The Roadmap

### 🌱 Phase 1 — Foundations (Week 1)
*What is this "prompt engineering" thing anyway?*

- [ ] What is an LLM? (tokens, context window, temperature — plain English)
- [ ] Why prompts matter: same model, wildly different outputs
- [ ] Anatomy of a good prompt: **Role + Task + Context + Format**
- [ ] Common beginner mistakes (vague prompts, missing context, no output format)

**🧪 Practice:**
- [ ] Prompt an AI to explain a topic to a 5-year-old vs. a PhD student — compare outputs
- [ ] Rewrite 5 bad prompts from your own history into well-structured ones

---

### 🌿 Phase 2 — Core Techniques (Weeks 2–3)
*The fundamentals every prompt engineer must know.*

- [ ] **Zero-shot prompting** — asking directly, no examples
- [ ] **Few-shot prompting** — teaching with examples
- [ ] **Instruction prompting** — clear, imperative commands
- [ ] **Delimiters & formatting** — using ```, """, <tags> to structure input
- [ ] **Output formatting** — JSON, tables, bullet points, word limits
- [ ] **Role/persona prompting** — "You are a senior Python developer..."

**🧪 Practice:**
- [ ] Get an AI to output valid JSON every time (no extra text!)
- [ ] Build a few-shot prompt for sentiment classification
- [ ] Create 3 personas (teacher, lawyer, comedian) and test the same question

---

### 🌳 Phase 3 — Intermediate (Weeks 4–5)
*Making the model think step by step.*

- [ ] **Chain-of-Thought (CoT)** — "Let's think step by step"
- [ ] **Self-consistency** — multiple reasoning paths, pick the best answer
- [ ] **Prompt chaining** — split big tasks into smaller sequential prompts
- [ ] **Prompt templates** — reusable, parameterized prompts
- [ ] **Conversation design** — multi-turn prompting, context management

**🧪 Practice:**
- [ ] Solve math/logic puzzles: direct prompt vs. CoT — measure the difference
- [ ] Build a 3-step prompt chain: idea → outline → full article
- [ ] Create one reusable template with placeholders like `{topic}`, `{tone}`

---

### 🌲 Phase 4 — Advanced (Weeks 6–7)
*Where prompting meets engineering.*

- [ ] **ReAct** — Reasoning + Acting (tools + prompts)
- [ ] **RAG basics** — Retrieval-Augmented Generation, grounding with your own data
- [ ] **Function calling / tool use** — structured outputs that trigger real actions
- [ ] **Tree of Thoughts** — exploring multiple solution branches
- [ ] **System prompts** — designing instructions for AI agents/apps

**🧪 Practice:**
- [ ] Write a prompt that makes an AI call a (mock) weather API via function calling
- [ ] Feed a document into context and ask questions only answerable from it (mini-RAG)

---

### 🛡️ Phase 5 — Evaluation & Security (Week 8)
*Trust, but verify.*

- [ ] **Prompt injection** — how prompts get hijacked (and how to defend)
- [ ] **Jailbreaking** — understand it to defend against it
- [ ] **Prompt evaluation** — how to test and compare prompts systematically
- [ ] Hallucinations: why they happen, how to reduce them

**🧪 Practice:**
- [ ] Try to prompt-inject your own chatbot → then fix it
- [ ] Build a tiny prompt test suite: 10 test cases, pass/fail rubric

